  "narration": {
    "voice": "american-male",
    "model": "mlx-community/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-Base-bf16",
    "revision": "a6eb4f68e4b056f1215157bb696209bc82a6db48",
    "referenceSha256": "b6647d4a6b70bf0b6f075d57d4199c5d51409a75ecdd7a0a2b09f419c42d5c52",
    "pacedAudioSha256": "faf902b5ff44beb56609cab6d876fefe8c99be4de834b02470906c3d5783db3f",
    "text": "My coding agent just finished implementing a feature. Let's see how Korus can help me review its work. Five thousand changed lines. Right here in the header, I can switch between the branch, uncommitted changes, and staged work. Before I review any of it, I exclude dependency lockfiles. They add a lot of noise. Now I have three hundred lines to focus on. The breakdown shows how much is code, tests, and documentation. I can quickly compare the implementation changes with the tests that support them. I open Changes and review each file type together. I can fold the groups I do not need and focus on the code. When something needs attention, I select the lines and add my feedback. My feedback carries the code and its location back into the conversation. Map gives me the shape of the change. Each tile is a file, and its size shows how many lines changed. The green and red bars along the bottom show added and deleted lines. I can filter by file type, then click a tile to jump straight to its diff. For the bigger picture, I generate a visualization. This helps me understand how the change affects the system, beyond individual files. Korus color-codes the parts that were added, changed, deleted, or left unchanged. I can follow those connections, then click through to the code I want to check. Coding with agents changes how I review code. Korus helps me cut through the noise, understand what changed, and make the final call.",
